

From: Kyrylo Tkachov <ktkachov@nvidia.com>

An RTL basic block that ends in a tablejump cannot normally be duplicated.
The instructions that compute the table address can be outside the block, so
copying and retargeting the table is not reliable.

Let an opted-in pass keep one table instead.  Before duplication, one jump
owns the adjacent table:

  jump A -> label T -> addr_diff_vec

After duplication, both jumps name the same label and table:

  jump A --\
             label T -> addr_diff_vec
  jump B --/

Mark the table with the sticky JUMP_TABLE_SHARED_P flag.  A shared table is
immutable because each entry describes a successor of every dispatch that
uses it.  Block deletion can remove one dispatch but must retain the table for
the others.

The table still follows one of the jumps through it.  When the block that
the table follows is deleted, move the table after another jump through it:

  jump A; label T; addr_diff_vec        (A deleted)
  ...                             ->    ...
  jump B                                jump B; label T; addr_diff_vec

So the insn before a table in use is still a jump through it, which is what
code that looks back from a table, or for the last insn of a function,
expects.  get_last_bb_insn includes a shared table only when the table
follows the block.  If no other jump uses the table, leave it to
delete_dead_jumptables.

Add PROP_rtl_allow_tablejump_duplication as a function property.  The RTL hook
uses it to permit the copy, retain JUMP_LABEL, and mark the table.  A pass can
set the property while it is prepared for the table to become immutable.

Guard the RTL redirection, deletion, fall-through, merge, and cross-jump paths
that assume one owner or physical adjacency.  Add checking assertions for
bulk deletion and verify every shared dispatch against the table successors.

Treat shared tablejumps as nonregular for interblock scheduling.  This prevents
selective scheduling from splitting shared-table edges to create loop
preheaders or bookkeeping blocks.  Likewise, do not let hw-doloop redirect
the entry edges of a hardware loop when one of them comes from a shared
tablejump.  Add shared_tablejump_p for these checks.

No pass opts in in this patch, so it does not change generated code by
itself.

gcc/ChangeLog:

	PR middle-end/126236
	* tree-pass.h (PROP_rtl_allow_tablejump_duplication): New property.
	* cfgcleanup.cc (outgoing_edges_match): Reject matches involving a
	shared jump table.
	* cfgrtl.cc (shared_jump_table_component_p): New function.
	(shared_jump_table_between_p): Likewise.
	(hosted_shared_table): Likewise.
	(other_table_user): Likewise.
	(move_table_after): Likewise.
	(delete_insn_chain): Assert that the range does not contain a shared
	jump table.
	(rtl_delete_block): Move a shared table that follows the block after
	another jump through it.
	(can_fallthru, rtl_can_merge_blocks, rtl_tidy_fallthru_edge): Do not
	remove or cross a shared jump table between basic blocks.
	(cfg_layout_can_duplicate_bb_p): Allow a tablejump when the new
	property is set.
	(duplicate_insn_chain): Retain a copied tablejump's table and mark it
	shared.
	(patch_jump_insn): Refuse to redirect through a shared table before
	creating a label for the new destination.
	(try_redirect_by_replacing_jump): Do not delete a shared table.
	(get_last_bb_insn): Include a shared table only if it follows the
	block.
	(force_nonfallthru_and_redirect): Likewise.
	(rtl_verify_edges): Check each shared table against the successors of
	each dispatch that uses it.
	* hw-doloop.cc (discover_loop): Reject a loop that is entered from a
	shared tablejump.
	* jump.cc (delete_related_insns): Do not delete a shared table.
	* rtl.h (JUMP_TABLE_SHARED_P): New macro.
	(rtx_def): Document the shared-table use of the unchanging flag.
	(shared_tablejump_p): Declare.
	* rtlanal.cc (shared_tablejump_p): New function.
	* sched-rgn.cc (is_cfg_nonregular): Reject shared tablejumps.
	* doc/cfg.texi: Document shared jump tables.
	* doc/md.texi: Likewise.
	* doc/rtl.texi (Flags): Document JUMP_TABLE_SHARED_P.
	(Insns): Document shared jump tables.

Signed-off-by: Kyrylo Tkachov <ktkachov@nvidia.com>
---
Changes since v3:
- While the CFG exists, a shared table that is in use now follows one
  of the jumps through it.  When rtl_delete_block deletes the block
  that the table follows, it moves the table and its label after
  another jump through it.
- Without the move, ia64 can lose the break.f and alpha can lose the nop
  after a noreturn call at the end of a function.  Both ports share
  tables in v4.
- get_last_bb_insn and force_nonfallthru_and_redirect now include a
  shared table that follows the block (v3 excluded all shared tables).
  So a new block does not go between the jump and its table.
- discover_loop (hw-doloop.cc) now rejects a hardware loop that a shared
  tablejump enters.  The ports cannot redirect such an edge.  Without
  the check, bfin and xtensa can ICE in force_nonfallthru_and_redirect.
- patch_jump_insn now refuses a shared table before it creates a label
  for the new destination, so a refused redirect adds no label.
- New functions: shared_tablejump_p (rtlanal.cc) and, in cfgrtl.cc, the
  static hosted_shared_table, other_table_user and move_table_after.
- is_cfg_nonregular and rtl_verify_edges now use shared_tablejump_p in
  place of the v3 open-coded test.  Their behavior does not change.
- Docs: cfg.texi, md.texi and rtl.texi (Insns) now state the placement
  rule.  rtl.texi (Flags) now documents JUMP_TABLE_SHARED_P and its use
  of the unchanging flag in addr_vec and addr_diff_vec.
- The subject now has [PR middle-end/126236].  The message and the
  ChangeLog describe the new code.
- No pass shares a table before patch 5.  Patch 5 tests the table move
  and the hw-doloop check: switch-dispatch-placement.c,
  switch-dispatch-hwloop.c and the bfin and xtensa switch-dispatch-1.c.

